<header><p class="enc-kicker">Place · site</p><h1 class="enc-title">Forteviot</h1></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Forteviot is a village in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/strathearn">Strathearn</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perthshire">Perthshire</a>, <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a>, that was a royal centre of the Pictish and early Scottish kings. The Pictish king Constantín son of Fergus is commemorated on the nearby <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/dupplin-cross">Dupplin Cross</a>, and Kenneth mac Alpin died at the royal residence there in 858. Excavations have revealed a major prehistoric ritual complex and an early medieval cemetery, and a carved stone arch from a royal church survives.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/forteviot.jpg" alt="Forteviot" width="320" height="141" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Anne Burgess · CC BY-SA 2.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Main_Square,_Forteviot.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Setting</h2><p>Forteviot, in Scottish Gaelic Fothair Tabhaicht, lies on the south bank of the River Earn between Dunning and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/perth">Perth</a>, in the council area of Perth and Kinross. The early royal site stood on Haly Hill, on the west side of the modern village, overlooking the Water of May. In 1991 the village had a population of 160.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>On 11 August 2009 archaeologists announced the discovery at Forteviot of a royal tomb of the early <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bronze-age">Bronze Age</a>. With the remains of the ancient ruler were a bronze and gold dagger, a wooden bowl and a leather bag, and archaeologists from the universities of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/glasgow">Glasgow</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/aberdeen">Aberdeen</a> continued to study the finds in the Strathearn Environs and Royal Forteviot (SERF) project.</p><p>The place is known to have been inhabited in the 9th century, and King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kenneth-macalpin">Cináed mac Ailpín</a> is said to have died in its palace. The ruins of a castle associated with Máel Coluim III were still visible in the 17th century. The Forteviot Arch, a monolithic sandstone arch of the early 9th century with figural sculpture, was found in an old bed of the Water of May and is now in the National Museum of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/scotland">Scotland</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/edinburgh">Edinburgh</a>; it probably adorned a royal chapel.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">The place today</h2><p>The present village was rebuilt in the 1920s as a model village designed by the architect James Miller for John Alexander Dewar, 1st Baron Forteviot, of the Dewar's whisky family, under the influence of the Garden City movement. Its village hall, opposite the main square, is a category A listed building. The parish church, dedicated to St Andrew, takes the form of a Georgian box chapel of 1778 and preserves several pieces of early medieval sculpture; its gravestones date from 1690.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Forteviot” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>Constantín son of Fergus, who ruled from 789 to 820 and may have controlled Dál Riata as well, is commemorated on the Dupplin Cross near the royal centre of <b>Forteviot</b>.</blockquote><a href="/empire/kingdom-of-the-picts">Kingdom of the Picts · Governance &amp; Law</a></li></ul></section>
